dblake@phy.ucsf.edu (David T. Blake) writes:

> tjk@ams.org writes:
> 
> >And in this case, xpdf doesn't quite cut it as a solution to
> >the AR previewer problem because it only uses the standard 13
> >fonts for display (and mathematical papers use lots of other
> >fonts than the standard thirteen).

The current release of xpdf (version 0.80) can't display embedded
fonts, as you say.  The next release will use t1lib (a free Type 1
rasterizer) to display Type 1 and Type 1C (or Type 2) fonts.

The current pdftops will embed Type 1 fonts in the PostScript file
(i.e., it will copy the embedded fonts from the PDF file into the PS
file).  The next release will convert Type 1C to Type 1 and embed
those, also.

Xpdf is, as far as I know, 64-bit clean.  I do a little bit of testing
on an Alpha system, and I know there are people using it on Alphas
(both DU and OpenVMS).

> Ghostscript does font-lookups for all fonts available on the
> machine (that are in the ghostscript directories). Adobe does
> it a little better in that it will perform somewhat intelligent
> substitutions for fonts that are not found.

Ghostscript will render embedded fonts.  I don't know exactly how it
substitutes for unavailable fonts.  Acroread uses multimaster fonts; I
don't think gs does.

- Derek



