I hate replying to my own postings, but I just came across this
on comp.sys.dec:

Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
From: "Karl N. Matthias" <karmat@matthias.org>
Subject: SUMMARY: XFree on DU 4.0
Date: Sat, 24 Apr 1999 00:58:16 -0400

	A number of people followed up on my post regarding XFree86 on
Digital UNIX.  It appears that no one has a running copy of XFree on
Digital UNIX (Tru64 or whatever).  I apparently got farther with it than
most people.  Here's what I can tell you about XFree86 on DU:

==You can compile all the non-server binaries without an immense amount of
effort.  This gives you cool things like color Xterms, xinit, etc.

==All core libraries can be compiled and installed which makes compiling
lots of X stuff much easier since it finds what it wants where it wants.

	From attempting compiles and of the Xservers it looks like you
will need at least gcc and gas in order to get even halfway through the
compile.  Digital/Compaq's compilers don't cut it (too bad) for the
server, though they work fine on everything else.  This is due to the way
in which assembly code is embedded in the C sources.
	Nasty x86 instructions are required for all the servers.  Yes,
they are (Linux Alpha gets around this by putting inb and outb in the
glibc and calling mmap or similar).  None of the drivers are free of x86
code except the Matrox/MGA driver.  With some ugly hacking you could
probably get a Matrox server compiled but it would be a lot of work for a
really good programmer/team.

SOLUTION:

	What I opted for was a little more expensive than I wanted, but it
saved a whole lot of time and now that I've been using it, I'm quite
pleased.  I had tried a large number of "supported" video cards with no
success beyond 640x480.  I found out, however, that the Elsa GLoria
Synergy 8MB card works under DU 4.0 D, E, and F.  I was at first daunted
by this because I thought it would be a lot of money (this is a high-end
CAD/CAM card), but I found out it was available from Microway for $130
+shipping(http://www.microway.com/).  They are an Alpha OEM company so
they will even know what you are talking about.  It's the same as you
would pay for a decent PC card, so it's not too bad, in my opinion!
	In order to get this card to work, you need to get the Open3D
drivers for this card from Digital. (thanks Tom Leitner!).  You can find
these at: http://www.service.digital.com/open3d/swelsa_GLoria_Synergy.htm
	Once these are installed, you need to restart dtlogin and it
should come up on the GLoria Synergy card.  On my machine it came up only
in 256 color mode so I hacked the /usr/lib/X11/Xserver.conf file with
this line near the bottom:

-pn -screen 1152x900 -vsync 76 -class TrueColor -vclass TrueColor

	I'm not sure if this is the best way to do this or not, but it
does work.  Anyway, I hope that helps other people who may be in the same
boat I was in.  I will say that the graphics output I'm seeing form this
card in terms of response, color, and clarity are about the best I've ever
seen.

Later!

Karl

