As a point of history, a long time ago, in an exurb far, far away (it
being May 19th...), there was a quick AD project that put the X10
server in kernel space.  I never saw it but it was described as
'quite responsive.'

> The procedure for accessing the video hardware could be somewhat
> different on Digital UNIX than on Linux, so the XFree86 code
> might not work unmodified. The XFree86 developers might be
> interested in helping you do the porting work, though.

Correct.  The /dev/ws0 mechanisms on DU shouldn't be compatible with
anything in XF86 land.  But, I'd love to hear about runtime efforts
to make it work on DU.  Basically, you either need to write xf86-
compatible device drivers or modify the os-related parts of the
server to work with du drivers.  There are things on DU which Linux
has no analog such as port/class interfaces for common functionality
abstraction and peer-peer interfaces between mouse serial device and
graphics devices.


