> The changes done to make this work were
> 
>   1.  The declaration
> 
>            void *threadFunc() throw(NewJunkException);
> 
>       was changed to
> 
>            void *threadFunc();
> 
>   2.  The declaration of the method body
> 
>            void *threadFunc() throw(NewJunkException)
> 
>       was changed to
> 
>            void *threadFunc()
> 
> The statement
>        throw NewJunkException();
> was NOT touched at all.
> 
> After this change, there is no problem. When compiled with g++ there is
> no problem even if these changes are not done.
> 
> How is it that it works with g++ and not with the C++ compilers of
> Solaris and Digital Unix?
> 
> I am not sure if it is a thread related problem or an exception related
> problem or if I am doing something silly.
> 
> Thanks in advance.

From the C++ team:

The reason that the program doesn't work on Compaq C++
is that when a program receives pthread cancel request, it's delived as
an exception.  The exception has no type-name right now that's
recognized by the C++ exception handling mechanism.  It can be caught
with catch(...) though.

The routine threadFunc has an exception specification that says only
NewJunkException can propagate out of it.  When pthread cancel is done,
the pthread-cancel exception is propagated, which is not expected by
the exception specification.  As a result, the C++ language routine
'unexpected' is called which aborts the program.

This is what we're supposed to do.  Someday, we are going to create types
for pthread exceptions but until we do, I don't think you can have
exception specification on routines which receive pthread cancel exceptions.
I also tried adding a "try { } catch (...) {}" around threadFunc, but I
think the rules are that you're supposed to propagate the pthread cancel
exception and not change it into another type of exception.

Possibly g++ implements pthread-cancel with a different mechanism than
the exception mechanism (maybe signals).   That's why it works for them.
Also, I tried Sun C++ 4.2 and they didn't hit the unexpected handler, so
I don't know what they do.

