With Compaq's assistance we installed the servers and software. There are
two shared SCSI interconnects with one storageworks shelf of disk each
between the two servers. We used LSM and AdvFS and the LSM volumes are
mirrored across the SCSI interconnects. Oracle and other application
software were installed on both servers. Then we configured the ASE
cluster. Then the application and it's database were configured for use.
The database is on the shared LSM volumes and is part of the defined
application disk service.

Startup and shutdown of the application and it's Oracle database is
controled by the customized script created for ASE.  The defined ASE
application disk service "app" is set up to call our custom script and it
lives in /usr/local/ase/bin/custom_script.
Of course it must be exactly the same on both servers.

The script must be able to handle the start up and shutdown of all
software and processes such as the Oracle instance and the TNS listener,
used by your application and especially those which may touch the shared
volumes. To that end we will kill processes if they do not terminate
properly because we cannot afford to wait for them.

If you create softlinks for the oracle database you may have to handle
them here as well.

Hope this helps.

