Sender: news@orga.com (News Administrator)
Organization: ORGA Kartensysteme GmbH
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi all,

I noticed a problem when I use two writes() to write first a header
containing the length of the message which I write after that.

The read() which reads the header finishes immediately. The read which I
use to get the actual data waits about constant 200ms before it
finished. In the example I sent 116 bytes as message.

Between the two reads I use a select with a timeout value before calling
the second read(). This select() needs also about 200ms before it says
that data are available. The read() then returns immediately. When I
don't use the select the same time of 200ms can be noticed directly in
the read() call.

The effect is constant on different DEC-Unix 4.0[abd] machines. One was
a low end dec alpha the other a high end dec alpha 4 times faster. Both
show exactly the 200ms delay.

When I use writev the 200ms delay is not there. Actually the delay is
below 100us with and without select() in between of the two reads.

Well I found out that the socket option TCP_NODELAY helps here. With
this option there is no delay even with two separat writes(). I wonder
why Digitial thought that after a second write() a third write() might
happen.


Bye,

    Aurel Balmosan.
I




