To all,

The process has started here at UNH to move all of our
administrative computing eventually from VMS to unix.
This is a daunting task as we have always been a Dec/Compac
OpenVMS shop on the administrative side of the house.

Although we don't know at this time what flavor(s) of unix
we may end up with, I suspect it may be Dec/Compac OSF/Digital
Unix/Tru64 Unix (what a mouth full), and/or perhaps Sun Solaris.
(thus the cross post to comp.unix.osf.osf1 and comp.unix.solaris)

The time table is at least a couple of years, so at this time
we are looking for general suggestions for investigation.

Question: What is out there in Unix Land that will give our
MIS and Sys Admin folks the tools to do all of those things
that we have on our VMScluster?

	Print queue management
	Backup/restore and media/device management
	Performance monitoring and advising
	Console management
	System administration
	Job scheduling

Question: Is it too much to ask for solutions that can deal
with VMS, and various unices (Tru64, Solaris, Linux, etc.),
and possibly NT (ugh).

Background: All adminstrative applications will eventually be
Oracle based products, so the support tools mentioned above
must get along with Oracle where applicable. Also, I'm
assuming that each major system will be on its own server,
so cross-platform capabilities are important, as are
cross-node cooperation, and central control.

