Gordon Burditt <gordonb.44lkz@sneaky.lerctr.org> wrote:
 |I am attempting to create a shared object under DU 4.0D for use with
 |TCL.  I'm not getting very far - it seems to link OK, possibly with
 |undefined symbol warnings, and then all I get out of dlopen()
 |and dlerror() is "cannot load ./sql.so". 
...
 |Is there any way to get dlopen() to be a LOT more verbose
...
 |% ld -shared -expect_unresolved "*" ... -lmysqlclient \
 |	-rpath /usr/lib:/usr/local/lib:/usr/local/lib/mysql:  -o sql.so

I have a possible answer to your question, and a follow-up question for the
group.

To get the loader to be more verbose:

   setenv _RLD_ARGS -trace

However, this may not help.  It didn't tell me anything useful (resolving
symX, resolving symY, mapping libA.so, then the same old dlopen failure
error for libA.so).

It turned out that OSF1 seems to be a bit broken in that it doesn't honor
RPATH settings in shared libraries.  In my case libA.so depends on libB.so.
libA was compiled with an RPATH which includes libB's location.  This
resolves just fine on Solaris and IRIX.  But no dice on OSF1.  It appears
this might be tripping up your build as well.

Resorting to the old LD_LIBRARY_PATH hack (augmenting it at runtime to
include the directory libA lives in) was the only reasonable way I could
find to get the dynamic loader to load libB.  Compiling a new RPATH into
the executable, the Python language interpreter, isn't an option (libA is a
Python module).

Two questions for the OSF1 experts: 

   1) Is there a way to get dependent shared library resolution to work
      without LD_LIBRARY_PATH?
 
   2) Why isn't an RPATH setting in a shared library used to find
      dependent shared libraries?

-- 

Randall Hopper
aa8vb@-nojunk-ipass.net





