Hi,

I see the same problem with Acrobat Reader 3.02 on out
Alphas running DU 4.0B.

It does indeed look like Adobe bungled the port to
the 64 bit architecture of the Alphas.

Well, that might not be the case.  It depends on how the
BBox numbers are stored in the embedded font.  If the font
is embedded as a CFF (/Type1C) font, then numbers between
-32,768 and 32,767 are stored as three bytes (one of which
is an escape byte) in a big endian fashion, and numbers in
the range �(2^31) � +(2^31�1) are stored as five bytes (one
of which is an escape byte) in a big endian fashion.  So it
depends on how the number -2954 is stored.

If it is stored as a 3 byte value (b0 b1 b2), the calculation
to get the desired number is (b1 << 8) | b2.  If this result
(a short int on most UNIX boxen) is stored in an int or a long,
the result is going to be treated as an unsigned short.

If it is stored as a 5 byte value (b0 b1 b2 b3 b4), the calculation
to get the desired number is:

   (b1 << 24) | (b2 << 16) | (b3 << 8) | b4

If this result (an int on most UNIX boxen) is stored in a long
(8 bytes on DU), the result will be treated as an unsigned int.

I am not sure if Adobe cast the bytes to ints, shorts, or longs
before bit shifting (if they used a bit shifting technique).
Perhaps they did, and it is a problem with promotion of types.

As for the font differences, I believe you are getting the
same fonts, they just are written out in a different order
on different platforms.

One can only hope that eventually, AR 4.0 for DU will
be released, and that this release will fix the problems.

Tom

