Robert Rader wrote:
> 
> To all,
> 
> The process has started here at UNH to move all of our
> administrative computing eventually from VMS to unix.
> This is a daunting task as we have always been a Dec/Compac
> OpenVMS shop on the administrative side of the house.
> 
> Although we don't know at this time what flavor(s) of unix
> we may end up with, I suspect it may be Dec/Compac OSF/Digital
> Unix/Tru64 Unix (what a mouth full), and/or perhaps Sun Solaris.
> (thus the cross post to comp.unix.osf.osf1 and comp.unix.solaris)
> 
> The time table is at least a couple of years, so at this time
> we are looking for general suggestions for investigation.
> 
> Question: What is out there in Unix Land that will give our
> MIS and Sys Admin folks the tools to do all of those things
> that we have on our VMScluster?
> 
>         Print queue management

UNIX (both Solaris and TRU64) and NT can submit jobs 
into each others print queues using at a minimum BSD LPD.

But I guess you are looking for a solution to manage all 
the print queues centrally. You could start by looking 
at Dazel which runs both client and server on Solaris 
and NT with clients for DU and a VMS gateway.

http://www.dazel.com

>         Backup/restore and media/device management

Veritas Netbackup or Legato Networker they both 
have distributed backup and restore and media 
management.

Veritas supports DU, Solaris and NT, there 
is a Veritas Netbackup Client for VMS.

Legato Support DU, Solaris and NT though not 
all the Legato unbundled poroducts are available 
for DU. Digital/Compaq have an OEM version of
Networker for OpenVMS.

http://www.veritas.com
http://www.legato.com
>         Performance monitoring and advising

Performance monitoring, depends on what you 
need. Sun bundles a product called Symon 
with all our Enterprise servers and provides 
a freeware product called Virtual-Adrian which
advises performance issues.

For a commercial performance management and 
capacity planning system you can look at

BMC Patrol and BMC Best-1 from BMC software,
there is a Patrol knowledge module for OpenVMS.

http://www.bmc.com

>         Console management

Again BMC who now own Boole and Babbage have a
product called Command Post 

http://www.bmc.com


>         System administration

Tivoli, CA Unicenter. Depending on what functionality
you need, policy based management, event management 
software distribution or not .

http://www.tivoli.com
http://www.ca.com

Both of these are frameworks that include a number of 
bundled products such as event managers, backup etc. 
However you may still need something like Veritas/Legato
since the bundled backup products may not have the 
functionality you require.

BMC/Veritas/Legato etc can all be integrated into Tivoli 
or CA.

If all you need is say user/account administration then 
you could do worse than NIS or NIS+ managed by a NIS/NIS+
manager such as AdminSuite on Solaris. 

>         Job scheduling
> 

Tivoli, CA Unicenter, Platinum Autosys 

http://www.platinum.com

> Question: Is it too much to ask for solutions that can deal
> with VMS, and various unices (Tru64, Solaris, Linux, etc.),
> and possibly NT (ugh).
> 

No but you need to be carefull about which products you select
and you also need to be aware of the fact that most of these 
products have a client and a server element, the client modules
are generally available for OpenVMS/NT but in many cases the 
server modules are not.

> Background: All adminstrative applications will eventually be
> Oracle based products, so the support tools mentioned above
> must get along with Oracle where applicable. Also, I'm
> assuming that each major system will be on its own server,
> so cross-platform capabilities are important, as are
> cross-node cooperation, and central control.
> 

BMC has an Oracle knowledge module which may be usefull
to you (don't know about Oracle RDB).

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect



