Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: UBPG
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

TCP/IP printing is available in the V4.0 releases however not supported
or documented until V4.0a.  The documentation is in release notes or
system admin guide depending on the os version. Setup instructions
provided below.

Tcp/ip printing provides more flexibility by using print filters where
lpd remote printing does not. Which print filter to use really depends
on your specific printing needs. A good starting point would be to use
the ps_level1of filter.

These directions are for Compaq Tru64 (Digital) UNIX V4.0 and later.

        1) An IP name and address must be obtained for the printer. Once
           you have an IP address refer to the printer documentation to
           set it in the printer.

           Let say for this example the IP name is pr.local.com.

           If you can ping the printer continue on.

        2) Now you need to know the socket address for that printer. HP
           printers use socket address 9100.

           As ROOT edit the /etc/services file and create a socket name.
           ie:
                port_9100       9100/tcp

           I chose the name port_9100 but any name can be used.

        3) Now that the services file has been updated, restart the inetd
           to load the changes. I don't believe this needs to be done
           but I have gotten into the habit of restarting inetd when
changing
           the services files. eg:

                # ps ax | grep inetd
                  446 ??       I        0:00.17 /usr/sbin/inetd
                  966 ttyp2    S  +     0:00.01 grep inetd
                # kill -HUP 446

          446 is the process ID number and will not be the same.

          ( You could also use the command "rcinet restart" )

         4) Now use lprsetup to create the print queue and make the
necessary
           printcap/printfilter changes. For this example the ps_level1
           printer was selected.

           Edit the printcap and change the lp value to:

                :lp=@pr.local.com/port_9100:\

           The ip name and port name were defined in the previous steps.

           Change the ct setting to `:ct=tcp:\'.

        5) If everything went well you should be able to print.

Example of a completed printcap entry:

pr|lp:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/ps_level1of:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lperr_pr:\
        :lp=@pr.local.com/port_9100:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/ps_level1of:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/print/pr:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:


