"John M. Rogers" wrote:
> [...] We recently have a new IBM RS/6000 to our servers.  Our sister
> company that owns the machine for benchmark testing wants the RS/6000
> to NFS export some of its directories/file systems.
> 
> I am concerned about any bandwidth impacts on my existing LAN/WAN users.
> 1)  I have heard that there is no impact while the server is idle - does
>     the exporting cause any traffic?

A little traffic, yes.  But no more than any other file-sharing protocol.

>     Do idle static mounts cause any traffic.

Only to receive notification that a cache record is out-of-date when a
file is updated by another system.  Relatively speaking, NFS is far
quieter that some other file-sharing protocols out there.

> 
> 2)  Do I need to worry about NFS performing any broadcasts on the LAN?

Not general broadcasts.  Notices of dirty cache blocks are targeted to
the machines that have it mounted.

> 
> 3)  Our home office MIS folks (pretty good Unix expertise) were quoted
>     that with disk being so cheap, why utilize your network bandwidth
>     to be used as a disk controller path.  WAN bandwidth is much more
>     expensive and is a recurring expense(monthly).  Any opinions on
>     this statement.

Then how do they share a common file among several client machines?  Are
they suggesting file or directory synchronizing utilities be run every
so often?  This actually causes more network bandwidth on the network
than a NFS mount would.  And the user would get delayed more often and
longer when accessing a file that has been determined out-of-sync,
because now the user has to wait for the file to be re-downloaded.  All
these file downloads to maintain synchronization require far more network
packets and bursts of network bandwidth than any remote mount would.

OK, so your MIS types might suggest a round-robbin approach, where a user
declares "I am in change of file X now, my changes only count", he ftp's
a file to his machine, updates it, and then declares "I am done now, you
can get the latest from my machine".  Nobody reads all of their notices,
and how do you deal with simultaneous access or updates?  Asking the users
to properly coordinate among themselves doesn't work.

If your MIS types are worried about security issues with UDP packets
(the type used by NFS), or the expense to make NFS work on MS-based PCs
(MS Windows or MS-DOS), then consider running SAMBA on the RS/6K.  This
implements Microsoft's SMB protocol that has a different access security
profile than NFS and can be more tolerant of WAN connection problems.

Note though that SMB is pretty much relegated to MS products and SAMBA.
NFS is more widely supported on a variety of platforms besides UNIX and
PCs, including DEC VMS, PrimeOS, and Apple-Mac.

-- 
Scott G. Hall
GTE Government Systems
North Carolina Systems Center
