On Thu, 6 May 1999 15:14:30 GMT, Scott G. Hall <Scott.Hall@GSC.GTE.Com> wrote:
>"John M. Rogers" wrote:
>> 3)  Our home office MIS folks (pretty good Unix expertise) were quoted
>>     that with disk being so cheap, why utilize your network bandwyidth
>>     to be used as a disk controller path.  WAN bandwidth is much more
>>     expensive and is a recurring expense(monthly).  Any opinions on
>>     this statement.
>
>Then how do they share a common file among several client machines?  Are
>they suggesting file or directory synchronizing utilities be run every
>so often?  This actually causes more network bandwidth on the network
>than a NFS mount would.

Whether the MIS advice is useful depends on the purpose of the
NFS setup --- if your goal is to share files, then having disks
local to each machine sounds like a very quesionable choice (*).
And if the goal is central administration/backup of files,
having a myriad of local disks is again quite questionable.

But if the goal was to save on disk space costs (by effectively
aggregating the free space on the various disks into a central
location), I'd agree that the NFS solution is quite likely to
be a false economy, and local disks make more sense.

(*) Unless you're planning on using one of the more experimental
    networked filesystems, such as XFS, in which case further study
    of the access patterns and network topography are needed in
    order to determine whether there would be a win over NFS.

		--Ken Pizzini



