Organization: Sumitomo Bank Capital Markets
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

You can read a quick and clear description of the roles of /etc/inittab
and the rc*.d directories
in section 4.1 of the "System Administration" guide, which can be found
at http://www.unix.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/ 

I would strongly recommend against fiddling with /etc/inittab; it is too
crucial for
the system booting properly that you not get it wrong. But putting a bad
script
in the rc*.d is no big deal; it will just not start your programs right.

Here is the quick scoop on what to do:

1) Master copies of the start scripts are stored in /sbin/init.d (e.g.,
we have one
   called samba, for starting you know what.)

2) If you want your script to run in multiuser network mode (3), create
a link in
 /sbin/rc3.d to the script in /sbin/init.d. Again, for samba, we have
  ln -s /sbin/init.d/samba /sbin/rc3.d/S89samba

3) If you want your programs to do some cleanup, or shutdown properly
when the machine
  changes init state (e.g., to 2, s or 0), create a link in the correct
rc*.d directory,
  but named KxxName, where xx is a suitable number.

4) Script contents: Your script should be a /bin/sh script. When called
as a "S" script,
  it will be given a single parameter "start"; when called as a "K"
script, "stop".
  I *strongly* recommend reading those in /sbin/init.d supplied by
Digital; take one as
  a model, and adapt it. Some products (samba) come with sample "system
5" startup scripts;
  these are also usually adaptable.

5) The number given to the links in 2) and 3) determines the order of
execution; for "S"
   scripts higher numbered scripts are executed later; you probably
don't want, e.g., samba
   to run before networking is up and running.

6) If there are machine dependent parameters you need, use the model of
the Digital scripts,
   and use rcmgr calls in the start script to query /etc/rc.config.
E.g., we have
   SAMBASSERVER="yes"
   export SAMBASERVER
  lines in our /etc/rc.config files on machines where we run samba, and
the /sbin/init.d/samba
  script looks for this. Read the man page for rcmgr for more. This way
you can keep the same
  startup scripts on all your machines, and emulate Digital's machine
localizations.

7) I *strongly* recommend you compare the scripts used by Digital Unix
with those on SunOS (kept
   in /etc/init.d, /etc/rc*.d) if you want to contrast engineering
practices. I defy anyone to 
   explain the Sun networking startup scripts in a seminar lasting less
than a week! Note how uniform
   in style and how clear the Digital scripts are.

8) Major Gotcha: The program started by your startup scripts MUST handle
SIGHUP; either explicitly
  or by ignoring it; init will send 2 SIGHUPS to the process; typically
a signal to your daemon
  (your programs must be daemons) to read its config files.


Hope this helps

