Dominique Kindt wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
> I am currently trying to repeat the last command(s) with the arrows on a
> Digital Unix v 4.0D. I know bash does it but I had some troubles with it
> so I decided not to use it anymore. Can anybody help me?

This depends on what shell you are using...

For csh, set the following in your (or the system wide) .login:

	set editmode=emacs
	set history=20

The history can be adjusted to however many commands you want to keep
track of. For Korn shell, set one of the following in your (or the
system wide) .profile:

	set -o emacs

	- or -

	export EDITOR=emacs

I'm not sure where to set the history list size on the latter. 

In both shells, these are dependent on whether or not you are using a
capable terminal/emulator. Replace emacs with vi for vi style command
editing.

Bourne shell does not support it, as far as I know.

Personally, this sounds like one of those little things that should be
in the Tru64/DUNIX FAQ... It appears to be very buried in the regular
documentation.
	
