In article <373707ad.5430639@192.9.200.16>, john.allen@geonet-tech.com (John Allen) writes:

>I've been using dump to backup data on my Alphastation running DU
>V4.0e.  Occasionally, the system will hang when I'm performing a dump
>during work hours(ie, when users are accessing files).
>
>I know that dump was originally intended to backup data in filesystems
>which are not mounted, and from single-user mode.  Does this still
>hold true?  Or, is there something I can do to run dump on mounted
>filesystems, while the system is in multi-user mode?

Running dump on an active file system will certainly contribute to
the overall I/O load.  The extra load may be enough to starve the
file system(s) on that disk from getting their fair share of I/O,
which is causing the pauses.  It could also be a very dirty cache
that's being written when the hangs happen or processes swapping.

If dump is pushing the file system over some threshold where file
system I/O is taking much longer, any backup utility will probably
add enough I/O to cause the same problem.  Some may add more.  While
dump isn't especially fast, it avoids doing some I/O that other
utilities do cause.

Because it works behind the back of the file system (so to speak)
it doesn't cause update of file access or inode modification times.
On a file system with lots of small files this can cause a lot of
extra I/O for backup utilities that go through the file system
interface.  At the other end of the file size spectrum, large files
may get the benefit of the file system doing read-ahead when other
utilities are used.  Dump just works away at a steady pace for small
and large files.  So other utilities may cause worse I/O problems
than dump.

To their advantage, these other utilities take advantage of the
buffer cache and simply won't do some I/O that dump has to.  Of
course this uses more buffer cache, which takes pages away from
other processes that will have to page more and thus cause even
more I/O.

I suspect the problem is not as much dump, but just doing backups
when the file system is already busy.

Still, other utilities you might try are:

vdump - Intended for AdvFS, but works fine on UFS.
tar   - More for archiving files than for backup, but it does the
        essential work.
cpio  - Same as for tar.

>Further, does anyone have any other suggestions for backup solutions
>for a small LAN?  Preferably, apps which allow backup of mounted
>filesystems, and filesystems on other hosts in the LAN.

Legato has Networker.  Veritas may have something.  IBM's ADSM
may do network backups.

Also, to the person suggesting clones if this is AdvFS...  Since
is using dump to backup the file system, it can't be AdvFS.  Dump
is specific to UFS.

>
